WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.920
A sound comes before juice, every time.

2
00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.920
Once a monkey learns that, when does a dopamine neuron fire?

3
00:00:10.983 --> 00:00:16.003
Wolfram Schultz's group recorded single dopamine neurons in the midbrain of monkeys.

4
00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:22.253
A fine electrode beside one neuron picks up its spikes, as the monkey gets drops of

5
00:00:22.253 --> 00:00:23.253
juice.

6
00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:28.347
In this picture, each row is one trial, and each tick one spike, lined up on an

7
00:00:28.347 --> 00:00:29.347
event.

8
00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:31.817
First, juice arrives with no warning.

9
00:00:32.777 --> 00:00:36.177
About three quarters of the recorded neurons fired a brief burst.

10
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.460
Then a sound began to predict the juice.

11
00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.020
Once the monkeys learned that, the burst came at the sound.

12
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.180
The juice itself, now expected, got little or no burst.

13
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.620
Why should the burst move?

14
00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:52.360
Picture one trial as a strip of moments.

15
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.620
Waiting, then the tone, a one-second delay, then the juice.

16
00:00:57.633 --> 00:01:01.213
Give each moment its own prediction of the reward still to come.

17
00:01:02.166 --> 00:01:05.346
Before the tone, the model sets the prediction to zero.

18
00:01:06.246 --> 00:01:07.646
That is a simplifying choice.

19
00:01:08.486 --> 00:01:10.926
The tone itself comes at unpredictable times.

20
00:01:11.966 --> 00:01:14.726
From the tone until the juice, the prediction is high.

21
00:01:15.866 --> 00:01:16.866
Juice is coming.

22
00:01:17.416 --> 00:01:19.796
After the juice, the prediction drops back.

23
00:01:20.656 --> 00:01:21.656
The trial is over.

24
00:01:22.566 --> 00:01:26.666
The error at each step is the reward that arrived, plus the change in the

25
00:01:26.666 --> 00:01:27.666
prediction.

26
00:01:28.116 --> 00:01:31.776
At the tone, no reward arrives, but the prediction jumps up.

27
00:01:32.476 --> 00:01:34.176
That jump is an error, and a burst.

28
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.140
During the delay, nothing changes.

29
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:39.140
No error.

30
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.990
At the juice, the reward arrives, but the prediction falls by the same amount.

31
00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:46.990
They cancel. No burst.

32
00:01:48.083 --> 00:01:53.263
So unexpected good news about the future makes an error, just as reward itself does.

33
00:01:54.233 --> 00:01:56.993
Now the tone sounds, and the juice never comes.

34
00:01:57.793 --> 00:01:59.373
What happens at the moment it was due?

35
00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:08.773
The prediction still falls, but no reward makes up for it.

36
00:02:09.513 --> 00:02:10.513
The error is negative.

37
00:02:11.583 --> 00:02:14.623
Firing pauses at about the time the juice was due.

38
00:02:15.563 --> 00:02:17.043
Hollerman and Schultz recorded it.

39
00:02:17.916 --> 00:02:22.816
In the model, add a light that comes at unpredictable times, always two seconds

40
00:02:22.816 --> 00:02:26.596
before the tone. After learning, the burst moves to the light.

41
00:02:27.566 --> 00:02:30.826
Now the tone is already expected, so it adds no error.

42
00:02:31.666 --> 00:02:34.386
The burst goes to the first cue nothing else predicted.

43
00:02:35.466 --> 00:02:38.646
A recording shows only that firing matches the error.

44
00:02:39.526 --> 00:02:40.826
Does the burst actually teach?

45
00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:47.090
In rats, a cue added alongside one that already predicts reward is normally learned

46
00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:52.010
poorly. A burst imposed with light at reward made them respond to it more.

47
00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:56.770
That shows an imposed burst is enough, not that natural bursts are needed.

48
00:02:57.783 --> 00:03:00.703
The error is the reward plus the change in prediction.

49
00:03:01.703 --> 00:03:04.843
After training, it comes at the first cue nothing else predicted.
